  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a\nridge of high pressure centered over far ERN Texas, a distinct \ninverted trough centered near the Big Bend of SW Texas and a \ndeveloping high center developing over Sonora Mexico. The inverted\ntrough looks impressive on WV imagery this morning with a \nmovement taking it toward SRN New Mexico today. As this shift \nhappens, it will promote the upper-high over Sonora Mexico to \nstrengthen with a weak col in the upper-flow developing along \nthe Arizona/New Mexico border. The latest GOES precipitable \nwater imagery indicated a dry-ish airmass across Southeast \nArizona, with PWATs 0.90-1.10 inches across Cochise/Graham and \nGreenlee, 1.10-1.30 inches across SE Pinal/ERN Pima and Santa \nCruz and 1.30-1.75 inches across central and western Pima.\n\nThe best larger scale lift will occur along the AZ/NM border\ntoday near this upper-level col. Even though the airmass is \ncurrently dry, there is a pocket of higher PWATs 1.25-1.50 \ninches over northern Chihuahua Mexico and south-central New \nMexico that will advect into far SE AZ late late this afternoon\nand into the evening. With this, I would expect more isolated \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms, especially over the \nhigher terrain along the AZ/NM border. With the drier mid- \nlevels, I would expect these storms to only be able to produce \nbrief moderate-heavy rain but more capable of producing gusty \noutflows to 40-45 mph. In fact, the 16/00Z HREF shows a 30-50 \npercent neighborhood probability of storm outflows 35+ kts in \nthe aforementioned area. \n\nOtherwise, The inverted trough stalls out across the bootheel of\nNM Monday but does force the upper-high center in Sonora Mexico\nto lift NW into the northern Baja. As this happens, a more \nfavorable area of deformation aloft develops across the eastern \nhalf of the forecast area. The airmass is still rather dry-ish \non Monday, but it should be enough to increase the coverage of \nstorms from isolated to widely scattered while expanding west \ninto ERN Pima and Santa Cruz. Heavy rainfall still is not a \nmajor threat with these storms, but the impact of gusty winds \nincreases Monday. This is supported by the 16/00Z HREF \nneighborhood probability of storm outflows of 35+ to 50-70 \npercent from Tucson northeast into Graham county.\n\nThe big weather story this week will be the return of the heat\nwith major HeatRisk returning to the majority of Southeast \nArizona. A Extreme Heat Warning is in effect Monday through\nFriday of the upcoming week for portions of Southeast Arizona.\n\nThe upper-high center will strengthen as it slowly drifts north\nduring the week, eventually making its way to near Las Vegas by\n21/00Z Thursday afternoon. This position will open Southeast \nArizona up to a more favorable easterly flow for thunderstorm \nactivity late this week into next weekend. The Monsoon season \nrolls-on despite the heat.",
